    "intro": "Leeks are among the most dependable alliums for a home garden, carrying the harvest from late summer through winter and, with the right varieties, into spring. They quietly gain size while summer crops fade, then provide sweet white shanks and useful green leaves when few fresh vegetables are available. Although closely related to onions, they are generally easier and more tolerant, especially in cool, moist conditions, but they still need a long season, steady moisture and some attention to blanching; most gardeners raise seedlings for transplanting.",
